 ## Security
 
-Every contract had a written security review before it reached mainnet. Slither found one issue that needed a fix, in the first OutfitMarket before it was deployed (its sale flag stayed raised during a buyer's ERC-1155 callback, so a buyer contract could move copies without retiring its own listings; fixed, and `contracts/test/OutfitMarket.t.sol` holds it), and nothing else that needed one (for BreederV2, HolderGift and OutfitMarketV2 it was run after their deploy, on 20 November). The findings that did need a fix are kept as regression tests in `contracts/test/SecurityReview.t.sol` and `contracts/test/OutfitMarketV2.review.t.sol`, which CI runs. The properties that matter most: every BEM fee is burned in the same transaction as its purchase, so no contract keeps a fee: a contract holds only what it owes others until they take it (in the second outfit market, each creator's, seller's and royalty share until its owner withdraws it, each red packet's USDT until it is opened or refunded, and the first market's OKB owed to the project); `expectedPrice` guards every purchase against a price changed in flight; listings bind a fingerprint of the pet's on-chain record so nothing in that record can change between an offer and its sale; the game asks for approval per pet, never for all (the adoption centre also honours an approval for all, if an owner gave one elsewhere); and the server is a mailbox and a relay, never the judge (the buyer's game verifies against the chain before paying). What each contract's owner can still change is listed, contract by contract, under the table of owner powers above.
+Every contract had a written security review before it reached mainnet. Slither found one issue that needed a fix, in the first OutfitMarket before it was deployed (its sale flag stayed raised during a buyer's ERC-1155 callback, so a buyer contract could move copies without retiring its own listings; fixed, and `contracts/test/OutfitMarket.t.sol` holds it), and nothing else that needed one (for BreederV2, HolderGift and OutfitMarketV2 it was run after their deploy, on 20 November). The findings that did need a fix are kept as regression tests in `contracts/test/SecurityReview.t.sol` and `contracts/test/OutfitMarketV2.review.t.sol`, which CI runs. The properties that matter most: all BEM moves straight from the buyer to the dead address and the parties in one transaction, so no contract keeps a fee: a contract holds only what it owes others until they take it (in the second outfit market, each creator's, seller's and royalty share until its owner withdraws it, each red packet's USDT until it is opened or refunded, and the first market's OKB owed to the project); `expectedPrice` guards every purchase against a price changed in flight; listings bind a fingerprint of the pet's on-chain record so nothing in that record can change between an offer and its sale; the game asks for approval per pet, never for all (the adoption centre also honours an approval for all, if an owner gave one elsewhere); and the server is a mailbox and a relay, never the judge (the buyer's game verifies against the chain before paying). What each contract's owner can still change is listed, contract by contract, under the table of owner powers above.
 
 Two limits of the mainnet contracts, and what the game does about them. A pet's name colour lives in the Extras contract, outside the record an adoption listing fingerprints, so a breeder could change it before a sale; the adoption centre therefore says that a name colour is not part of the price. A red packet "for pet owners" asks the registry that the claimer owns a settled pet (lived on chain three days and saved its third day), which stops casual farming but not someone willing to settle many pets; the game says exactly that where the option is chosen. (RedPacket's own source comment calls that check a day old and one day saved; the registry's rule, which is what runs, is three days lived and a saved day 3 or later, so one save can be enough.)
 
